WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:24.355 --> 00:00:26.323
(Piano music)

00:00:41.105 --> 00:00:44.475
I see trees

00:00:46.410 --> 00:00:48.179
Of green

00:00:51.615 --> 00:00:53.684
Red roses too

00:00:57.354 --> 00:00:59.356
I see them bloom

00:01:01.425 --> 00:01:04.395
For me and you

00:01:07.198 --> 00:01:10.234
And I think to myself

00:01:14.505 --> 00:01:18.809
What a wonderful world

00:01:29.153 --> 00:01:31.455
All the colors of the rainbow

00:01:32.923 --> 00:01:35.025
So pretty in the sky

00:01:37.094 --> 00:01:42.266
Are also on the faces of people going by

00:01:44.268 --> 00:01:49.006
I see friends shaking hands saying,

00:01:49.006 --> 00:01:51.342
Saying, "How do you do?"

00:01:53.944 --> 00:01:56.614
They're really saying

00:01:58.215 --> 00:02:01.252
I love you

00:02:15.199 --> 00:02:19.436
I hear babies crying

00:02:22.873 --> 00:02:25.075
I watch them grow

00:02:26.510 --> 00:02:33.317
They'll know much more than I'll ever know

00:02:38.756 --> 00:02:42.860
And I think to myself

00:02:46.897 --> 00:02:50.901
What a wonderful

00:02:54.505 --> 00:02:56.240
World

00:03:06.083 --> 00:03:08.485
(Music ends)

00:03:09.787 --> 00:03:16.660
(Applause and cheers)

00:03:18.295 --> 00:03:20.164
Thank you very much, everybody.

00:03:20.731 --> 00:03:22.299
Isn't that a beautiful song?

00:03:22.700 --> 00:03:24.034
I just love it.

00:03:24.034 --> 00:03:27.271
You know, music is a language
you already know.

00:03:27.271 --> 00:03:29.940
And there's something
deeply powerful that happens

00:03:29.974 --> 00:03:31.742
when we come together to make it.

00:03:31.775 --> 00:03:33.277
Something connective,

00:03:33.310 --> 00:03:36.513
something transformative,
something vibrational.

00:03:36.513 --> 00:03:38.315
It's a feeling that moves us.

00:03:38.349 --> 00:03:40.718
Now I have a name for this feeling.

00:03:41.552 --> 00:03:44.088
I call it the wiggle.

00:03:44.455 --> 00:03:45.689
(Laughter)

00:03:46.790 --> 00:03:49.827
Now what’s the difference
between a wiggle and a straight line?

00:03:49.827 --> 00:03:52.763
Well we all know
how a straight line feels, right?

00:03:52.796 --> 00:03:54.465
Rigid, unyielding,

00:03:54.498 --> 00:03:56.433
inflexible, linear.

00:03:56.467 --> 00:03:58.669
Like a Roman road or the hand of a clock.

00:03:58.702 --> 00:04:00.604
It's set, it's certain.

00:04:01.739 --> 00:04:06.410
But the wiggle, on the other hand,
is soft and flexible and fluid.

00:04:06.877 --> 00:04:10.381
The wiggle is smooth
and constantly in motion, right?

00:04:11.048 --> 00:04:13.984
So where the straight line
is firm and brittle,

00:04:14.018 --> 00:04:16.320
the wiggle is supple and resilient --

00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:18.922
like water -- constantly changing shape,

00:04:18.956 --> 00:04:21.225
but stronger than almost anything.

00:04:21.258 --> 00:04:25.663
As the great Alan Watts used to say,
there are no straight lines in nature.

00:04:26.630 --> 00:04:30.267
Now I've learned many
of the principles of wiggledom

00:04:30.267 --> 00:04:32.002
through the process of making music

00:04:32.036 --> 00:04:34.371
because music is inherently wiggly.

00:04:34.405 --> 00:04:37.274
Music is made out of a bunch
of those things, there.

00:04:37.308 --> 00:04:39.877
Essentially, music is just wiggling air.

00:04:39.910 --> 00:04:41.545
That's what's going on with music.

00:04:41.578 --> 00:04:42.846
And as musicians,

00:04:42.846 --> 00:04:46.083
we are constantly oscillating
between different states, right?

00:04:46.116 --> 00:04:48.619
So high to low

00:04:48.652 --> 00:04:50.954
Or loud to quiet.

00:04:52.122 --> 00:04:55.426
Thick to thin.

00:04:57.294 --> 00:04:59.229
Music is constantly in motion.

00:04:59.263 --> 00:05:02.032
And everything in music
is learned through play.

00:05:02.533 --> 00:05:06.470
Now there are three essential
types of wiggle in music

00:05:06.503 --> 00:05:08.472
that I'd like to talk to you about today.

00:05:08.472 --> 00:05:10.741
The first is called melody,
what we call melody.

00:05:10.774 --> 00:05:14.078
Now a melody draws a line
from one place to another

00:05:14.078 --> 00:05:16.080
and tells a story as it goes.

00:05:16.080 --> 00:05:20.084
So Louis Armstrong walks down the street,
looks around and he goes,

00:05:20.117 --> 00:05:21.819
"I see trees of green."

00:05:21.852 --> 00:05:23.087
Yeah?

00:05:23.087 --> 00:05:25.422
Not, "I see trees of green."

00:05:25.456 --> 00:05:26.657
Not that.

00:05:26.657 --> 00:05:28.359
"I see trees of green."

00:05:28.792 --> 00:05:30.027
It's a tree.

00:05:30.594 --> 00:05:32.663
"I see skies are blue."

00:05:33.063 --> 00:05:34.665
It's the sky, right?

00:05:35.199 --> 00:05:37.935
"Clouds are white,"
there's a little cloud up there.

00:05:38.402 --> 00:05:39.737
"The bright blessed day,

00:05:39.737 --> 00:05:41.038
The dark sacred night."

00:05:41.038 --> 00:05:42.873
Is that not the sun going down?

00:05:42.906 --> 00:05:44.408
It is.

00:05:44.441 --> 00:05:47.578
A good melody arc tells a story
and draws a line that moves us.

00:05:47.611 --> 00:05:49.546
That's the first kind of wiggle.

00:05:49.980 --> 00:05:53.417
Now the second kind of wiggle
is what they call rhythm.

00:05:53.417 --> 00:05:57.054
Now rhythm is about time,
but it's also about the body.

00:05:57.087 --> 00:05:58.589
So Louis does not sing this:

00:05:58.622 --> 00:06:00.157
"I see trees of green.

00:06:00.190 --> 00:06:02.726
Red roses too, I see --"

00:06:02.760 --> 00:06:04.061
No, sir.

00:06:04.395 --> 00:06:06.864
Instead he goes, "I see trees green."

00:06:06.897 --> 00:06:10.200
(Vocalizing)

00:06:11.335 --> 00:06:12.603
Right?

00:06:12.603 --> 00:06:14.271
I just saw a bunch of you do this.

00:06:14.938 --> 00:06:17.808
Rhythm physically moves the body.

00:06:17.808 --> 00:06:19.443
And with that motion and play,

00:06:19.476 --> 00:06:21.945
the melody has something
to be in conversation with.

00:06:21.979 --> 00:06:23.480
And suddenly we have space,

00:06:23.480 --> 00:06:26.450
and we have form, and we have
proportion and dynamics

00:06:26.450 --> 00:06:28.419
and that kind of storytelling.

00:06:28.452 --> 00:06:29.753
It's beautiful.

00:06:29.787 --> 00:06:32.856
So rhythm moves us
and our melody through time.

00:06:32.890 --> 00:06:34.758
That's the second kind of wiggle.

00:06:34.792 --> 00:06:38.495
Now the third kind of wiggle
is my personal favorite,

00:06:38.529 --> 00:06:40.497
and it's what they call harmony.

00:06:40.497 --> 00:06:43.167
Now harmony is about relationships.

00:06:43.534 --> 00:06:45.469
It's the wiggles between the wiggles.

00:06:45.936 --> 00:06:48.672
And by adding these chords,
little combinations of notes,

00:06:48.705 --> 00:06:50.274
like clusters of notes,

00:06:50.274 --> 00:06:53.076
we get to take our melody and our rhythm

00:06:53.110 --> 00:06:55.913
and decide how we want
these things to feel.

00:06:55.946 --> 00:06:59.349
OK, so with this song, we start home.

00:06:59.383 --> 00:07:01.351
We begin home, right?

00:07:04.655 --> 00:07:05.923
Begin to move.

00:07:07.524 --> 00:07:09.526
Somewhere close by, quite warm.

00:07:11.562 --> 00:07:12.896
A bit of darkness there.

00:07:14.998 --> 00:07:16.266
Lightening.

00:07:17.334 --> 00:07:18.469
Saddening.

00:07:19.403 --> 00:07:21.438
And then something extraordinary happens.

00:07:21.805 --> 00:07:23.874
Our melody note stays the same.

00:07:23.907 --> 00:07:26.944
"And I think to myself,"
right, one melody note.

00:07:27.411 --> 00:07:30.514
But the chord, which
could happily stay here,

00:07:30.547 --> 00:07:31.748
becomes this.

00:07:31.782 --> 00:07:36.320
"And I think to myself,"
did you feel the wiggle?

00:07:37.020 --> 00:07:38.288
It's special, isn't it?

00:07:38.322 --> 00:07:41.258
This note just completely changed color.

00:07:42.759 --> 00:07:43.894
Didn't it?

00:07:43.927 --> 00:07:45.462
That's the power of harmony.

00:07:45.496 --> 00:07:48.298
So with a chord, I can squint,

00:07:48.332 --> 00:07:50.267
I can reassure,

00:07:50.300 --> 00:07:52.503
I can enchant,

00:07:52.536 --> 00:07:55.572
I can purify,

00:07:55.606 --> 00:07:57.040
I can terrify.

00:07:59.643 --> 00:08:01.678
So that when I get home, in the end ...

00:08:04.114 --> 00:08:07.217
It’s all the more beautiful
because we’ve traveled somewhere new.

00:08:07.251 --> 00:08:09.152
Harmony tells an emotional story

00:08:09.186 --> 00:08:11.622
through tension, release,
dissonance and consonance

00:08:11.655 --> 00:08:14.358
by combining many
different notes together.

00:08:14.391 --> 00:08:15.859
That's the third wiggle.

00:08:15.893 --> 00:08:19.463
So you may say, “But Jacob,
why does it make us wiggle so?

00:08:19.997 --> 00:08:22.466
How do these notes
make us feel these things?”

00:08:22.499 --> 00:08:26.036
And I would say, well, because music
is about so much more than just notes.

00:08:26.036 --> 00:08:27.604
Music is about life.

00:08:27.638 --> 00:08:30.607
In music, we learn that being
in harmony with the world

00:08:30.641 --> 00:08:33.677
doesn't mean everyone is singing
the same note at the same time.

00:08:33.677 --> 00:08:36.947
It's about embracing the dissonance
and following the heart to home.

00:08:37.548 --> 00:08:39.816
We learn that being in time with the world

00:08:39.850 --> 00:08:41.852
doesn't have to mean stepping into line.

00:08:41.852 --> 00:08:44.755
It's about motion and knowing
when your time is right.

00:08:44.788 --> 00:08:46.623
Leaving space for the rest.

00:08:46.957 --> 00:08:50.427
We learn that being in tune
is not about being perfect.

00:08:50.928 --> 00:08:52.462
It's about following a line

00:08:52.496 --> 00:08:54.531
and being open to where it might take you.

00:08:54.531 --> 00:08:56.300
Because here's the truth of it.

00:08:56.333 --> 00:08:58.569
Life does not work in straight lines.

00:08:58.602 --> 00:09:01.605
Life is full of curveballs,
full of ups and downs.

00:09:01.638 --> 00:09:04.408
And either we yield
and we bend and we soften.

00:09:04.875 --> 00:09:06.843
Or we stiffen up and we break.

00:09:07.411 --> 00:09:09.746
So when the world
feels like a straight line,

00:09:09.780 --> 00:09:11.081
be a wiggler.

00:09:11.114 --> 00:09:12.549
It's in your nature, trust me.

00:09:12.583 --> 00:09:14.318
You already know how to wiggle.

00:09:15.452 --> 00:09:16.954
You just need to remember.

00:09:17.988 --> 00:09:20.390
(Piano music)

00:09:34.037 --> 00:09:36.039
I think to myself,

00:09:37.307 --> 00:09:41.612
What a wonderful world

00:09:46.583 --> 00:09:48.085
Yes

00:09:49.486 --> 00:09:51.989
I think to myself

00:09:57.694 --> 00:10:01.865
What a wonderful

00:10:06.069 --> 00:10:08.205
World

00:10:08.238 --> 00:10:10.073
World

00:10:10.073 --> 00:10:11.608
World

00:10:11.642 --> 00:10:14.645
(Audience sings)

00:10:16.947 --> 00:10:18.148
(Singing ends)

00:10:19.383 --> 00:10:26.323
(Cheers and applause)

